{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>All the streams.</b> Even though the streams go to the sea, it will not be full until the decree passes and covers the earth, because the waters that went to it — they return to go to their place, without end. For vapor constantly rises from the sea to the firmament, and they are most of the clouds, and the fresh waters rise because of their lightness, and the vapor returns as rain, like the one who calls to the waters of the sea and pours them out. And the waters of the springs come from the rain, and all the streams come from the springs. Do you not see that in a year of drought most of the springs dry up? And it is written: \"and the stream dried up, for there was no rain in the land.\" This is the matter of \"to the place\" — where the streams go. And Solomon did not explain the cause of the return of the waters to their place, just as he did not explain the cause of the return of the sun from west to east — whether above the firmament, or along the sides, or by way of the earth being round and at the center of the upper sphere — for these matters require proofs, and a foundational account to speak about every matter visible to the eye does not require proofs. And the point in mentioning these four — they are the sun, which is the place of fire, and the wind, and the waters, and the earth — is that all existent things under the sun are born from them: plants and animals and human beings and birds and fish of the sea. And if they — which are the roots — their way is to return to their place as they were, how can what is born from them endure? And if they were in their beginning hevel, so they will be in their end. And if man is hevel, all the more so his deed, which is like an accident to him, and all the more so his thoughts, which are like an accident of an accident, as in \"HaShem knows the thoughts of man, that they are hevel.\""], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The Hebrew רובי המעיינים read literally as 'most of the springs'; the form רובי is unusual but the sense is clear. The phrase כענין הקורא למי הים וישפכם is elliptical and obscure; translated hyper-literally. The verse citation alluded to at the end is Psalms 94:11."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:59.709631453Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}